    announcement: 'Bots can now run a whole browser task in one step. In testing, two tracking lookups went from 8 steps and 50 seconds to 1 step and about 4 seconds, with the same answer.',
    agent: 'Use the browser script tool whenever browser work would take more than two or three calls. Pass steps, a fixed list (never code): open {url, keep?}, tab {url_contains}, goto {url}, wait {text | selector | gone | url_contains | ms}, click {selector | text (+role, exact, nth) | x,y, optional?}, fill {selector | label, value, submit?}, press {key}, select {selector | label, option}, scroll, and the reading steps text, table, links, attr, exists, url. Names copied from read work as-is for click text and fill or select label, including a required marker or icon name. Each step waits a few seconds for its element, so put the read step in the same script rather than a wait followed by a separate read. With no open or tab step the script uses the open page only when a single web page tab exists; otherwise begin with tab. Give text steps a selector so only the needed part returns; output is capped and flagged truncated. Tabs the script opens close when it ends unless keep:true; use tab to continue on a page that is already open or signed in. Keep read, click and find for single steps and unfamiliar pages. Never put a password, token or code in a step: use fill_secret, fill_totp, fill_sms_code or fill_email_code as separate calls, then continue with a tab step; page-reading steps are refused while a just-filled secret is live. Steps stop at the first failure and nothing is replayed: when error.outcome_unknown is true, check the page before repeating a click or keystroke. A page dialog stops the script and leaves its tab open for the dialog tool. A script grants no authority: every purchase, send and other business effect keeps its existing approval, claim and duplicate guards. Guide: /#/bot-guide?feature=browser-scripts.',
